# Departure Board Project

## Overview

This project is a fork of the excellent [Bus Departure Board](https://github.com/jfoot/Bus-Departure-Board) created by Jonathan Foot. His innovative work in creating a physical departure board using a Raspberry Pi and LED display has been the foundation and inspiration for this enhanced version.

This personal project represents a comprehensive refactoring and expansion of the original codebase, developed with the assistance of Claude AI to improve architecture and implement new features.

### Enhancements
- Simplified codebase with improved modularity and abstraction
- Streamlined service implementation interface for easier integration of new transport APIs
- Enhanced error handling and recovery mechanisms
- Optimized display rendering and animations
- Improved configuration management

### Current Services
- Transport for London (TfL) - Live bus and tube departures

### New Features
- RESTful API for remote board control and station management
- iOS companion app:
  - Interactive London transport map
  - Station and stop discovery
  - Real-time departure information
  - Remote board control through REST API integration
  - Intuitive station selection and display management
  - You can found more info about the iOS app on this repo:[Next Stop App](https://github.com/gui1207/tbd)

## Raspberry Pi Setup

### Hardware Requirements
- Raspberry Pi Zero 2W
- 3.2-inch OLED Display (256x64 SPI)
- Power Button (Optional)
- Power Supply - UPS HAT (Optional)

### System Preparation

#### 1. Operating System
- Install Raspberry Pi OS Lite (64-bit)
- Configure SSH and Wi-Fi during installation

#### 2. System Configuration
```bash
# Update system
sudo apt update && sudo apt full-upgrade -y

# Enable SPI interface
sudo raspi-config
# Navigate to: Interfacing Options > SPI > Enable

# Add user to SPI and GPIO groups
sudo usermod -a -G spi,gpio pi

# Install system dependencies
sudo apt install -y \
  python3-dev \
  python3-pip \
  build-essential \
  libopenjp2-7 \
  libfreetype6-dev \
  libjpeg-dev \
  libtiff6

# Optional - only for development with pygame emulator
sudo apt install -y \
  libsdl2-dev \
  libsdl2-image-dev \
  libsdl2-mixer-dev \
  libsdl2-ttf-dev
```

### Project Setup

#### 1. Clone Repository
```bash
git clone [YOUR_REPOSITORY_URL]
cd departure-board
```

#### 2. Create Virtual Environment
```bash
python3 -m venv venv
source venv/bin/activate

# Install Python dependencies
pip install -r requirements.txt
```

#### 3. Run the Application
```bash
# Test run
python3 main.py -k YOUR_API_KEY -s STATION_ID 
```

#### 4. Auto-start Configuration
Create systemd service file and configure auto-start:

```bash
# Create service file
sudo nano /etc/systemd/system/departure-board.service

# Add configuration to file:
[Unit]
Description=Departure Board Service
After=network.target

[Service]
ExecStart=/home/pi/departure-board/venv/bin/python3 /home/pi/departure-board/main.py -k YOUR_API_KEY -s STATION_ID -e none
WorkingDirectory=/home/pi/departure-board
StandardOutput=inherit
StandardError=inherit
Restart=always
User=pi

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target

# Enable and start service
sudo systemctl enable departure-board
sudo systemctl start departure-board

# Check status
sudo systemctl status departure-board
```

#### 5. Display Shutdown 
Create systemd service file to shutdown the diplay:

```bash
# Create service file
sudo nano /etc/systemd/system/display-shutdown.service

# Add configuration to file:
[Unit]
Description=Display Shutdown Service
DefaultDependencies=no
Before=shutdown.target reboot.target

[Service]
Type=oneshot
ExecStart=/home/pi/departure-board/venv/bin/python3 /home/pi/departure-board/display-shutdown.py
TimeoutSec=5

[Install]
WantedBy=shutdown.target reboot.target

# Enable and start service
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl enable display-shutdown

# Check status
sudo systemctl status display-shutdown
```

### Troubleshooting
- Verify SPI connection
- Check API key and station ID
- Ensure all dependencies are installed

